KB-277 D21 probe (permission policy: trailing-* band first-match-wins,
recovered denial keeps success).

This step's allow_tools is exactly ["s*", "shell"]: allow bands match the
POLICY TOOL NAME, and the first matching band wins. Your bash calls map to
the policy tool `shell`, which the trailing-* band `s*` matches first.

Make exactly this three-call sequence. Call (2) is ENGINEERED to be denied;
the denial is NOT a step failure — you recover and the step still finalizes
success (a recovered denial keeps the turn's success). The step's approved
outcome carries a payload schema, so the finalize MUST use the typed payload
lane.

(1) Call the bash tool now, exactly once:

        command: printf d21-shell-band-ok > ./d21_shell_band_record

    This runs: its policy tool name `shell` matches the `s*` band, which is
    the FIRST matching band even though the later exact `shell` entry would
    also match (the grant echoes the winning pattern in run events). The
    record lands in this step's working directory (the probe root the
    harness points the shell environment at).

(2) Call the view tool now, exactly once, on the file ../harness_contracts_v1/main.chcl

    This is DENIED (its policy tool name `read` matches neither allow band);
    expect a permission denied result telling you the tool was not allowed.

(3) Call the submit_outcome tool now, exactly once — this step's approved
    outcome declares a reason payload schema, so use the payload lane:

        outcome = "approved"
        payload = {"reason": "D21 recovered denial keeps success: the trailing-* band granted the shell tool call first-match-wins (pattern s* echoed in the permission.granted event), the read tool call was denied with a permission.denied event naming the tool, and the step still finalized approved after recovering"}
        comment = "D21 permission band probe"

No other tools, no other calls.